1. History:  
Mount starts with devices D1, D2, D3 (w floor=2). Reservation succeeds. Transaction starts, takes number, writes code 1 unit. Publish stage: root slot write to D1 fails (transient). Switch attempt begins. During switch, writing new instance id to superblocks fails due to D2 failure; device count drops to 1 (below w floor). System treats this as transient failure (incorrectly), increments N_switch to 1. Another switch attempt occurs; D3 fails, device count=0. System counts N_switch=2. N_switch=2 is set to max allowed. Third switch attempt triggers read-only. But correct behavior is immediate read-only when device count drops below w floor during switch attempt. Rule fails to check resource failure (device count) before proceeding with switch attempts. Smallest change: check device count before switch attempt; if below w floor, go read-only immediately without counting switch attempts.  

2. History:  
Mount starts with devices D1, D2, D3. Reservation for switch blocks succeeds. Transaction starts, takes number. During publish, root slot write to D1 fails (transient). Switch attempt uses reserved blocks to write new instance id to superblocks. D1 fails during write, but blocks are marked used. Another transient failure occurs (root slot write to D2 fails). System attempts switch again but reservation is already consumed (no blocks left). System fails to reserve blocks for second switch attempt. Rule states reservation is done before number taken; if reservation fails, go read-only. But here reservation was consumed by first switch attempt, and second switch attempt fails to reserve (no blocks), so should go read-only. However, system may incorrectly attempt switch again (treating as transient) without checking reservation status. Smallest change: track reservation status per transaction; if reservation was used but not released, fail switch attempt and go read-only.  

3. History:  
Root ring has K=2 roots. Current mounted instance is I2. Recovery chooses root from I2 (T_pub=5). Instance I1 has records applied by replay: I1's W=0 per rule. But replay applies records from I1 because a rollback row for I1 exists with W=3 (from earlier recovery). Rule states "every instance other than the chosen root's has W=0" but rollback row for I1 has W=3. During replay, system applies I1's records up to W=3. Rule fails because rollback rows can have W>0 for non-chosen roots. Smallest change: in replay lower bound, honor rollback row's W for any instance, not just chosen root's instance.  

4. History:  
Code 3 unit for identity X is written at checkpoint 10 by instance I1 (birth=10). At checkpoint 11, instance I1 writes another code 3 unit for X (birth=11). Both are readable after recovery. Rule states "the pair (birth, instance id) for code 3" as per-class key. But two versions of X exist with same instance id I1 but different birth (10 and 11), so they have different keys. However, if a clone operation creates a new code 3 unit for X with birth=10 and instance I1, but the container index doesn't include it, and ancestry table points to original birth=10. During visibility check, both versions have (birth=10, I1) as key. Rule fails to prevent duplicate keys for same identity. Smallest change: ensure code 3 container index includes all versions, using (birth, instance id, write sequence) as key.  

5. History:  
Majority set: D1, D2, D3. New instance id=5 written to all superblocks. D1 is offline during write. System marks D1 as missed take-a-number. Later, D1 returns. Write-off rule writes D1 off entirely. But D1 never missed any take-a-number (only offline during write). System permanently marks D1 read-only. Rule states "device that missed a take-a-number is written off" but D1 didn't miss any; it was offline during write. Majority exclusive open succeeded (D2, D3 online), but D1 was not part of majority set during write. Write-off rule incorrectly applies to D1. Smallest change: only write off devices that failed to participate in majority write during id assignment.  

6. History:  
Rollback row exists for instance I1: (r_old, T_old, 0) with rollback flag. Replay lower bound applies I1's records only up to W=0. But a later transaction writes a new record for I1 with transaction number=1. During recovery, replay lower bound ignores it because W=0. However, the rollback row was created for an earlier aborted transaction; the new record is valid. Rule refuses valid record forever. Smallest change: allow replay to apply records above W only if rollback flag is not set for that instance.
